# Git configuration for the IN-CONTAINER test run only.
#
# Passed as GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL by build/tasks/Test.build.ps1. It is NOT baked into either image:
# the leash image is the product, not a test fixture, and it has no business carrying a git
# exception that exists so a suite can run against a bind mount.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
#
# /work is a bind mount, so every file in it carries the HOST uid rather than the container's.
# git refuses to operate in a repository owned by another user - "detected dubious ownership",
# exit 128 - which took the BeforeAll of every git-dependent suite down with it and read as 25
# failures rather than as one configuration problem.
#
# WHY NOT GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY/VALUE, WHICH WAS TRIED FIRST
#
# Those arrive in git's "command line" scope. That is enough for ordinary commands - it fixed
# `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` - but `git clone /work ...` still refused, and
# `git config --list --show-origin` shows why it is not a mystery: the entries are present and
# simply not honoured for that path. safe.directory is deliberately restricted in which scopes
# it may come from, so that an ad-hoc `-c` cannot switch the protection off. A global config
# file is a scope it is honoured from.
#
# WHY TWO ENTRIES AND NOT `directory = *`
#
# The wildcard would cover every repository on the machine to buy a fix for one. `git clone`
# resolves the gitdir and checks ownership on /work/.git as well as /work, so both are named
# explicitly and nothing else is.
[safe]
	directory = /work
	directory = /work/.git
